    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d74">

                <pb facs="0147" n="71" sameAs="frus:frus1916/pg71"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.00/2370</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minister Reinsch to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-05T20:00:00+08:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 5, 1916, 8 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Prospects rather more favorable that internal peace may be restored. Hostilities
                    are suspended temporarily and negotiations are evidently proceeding with
                    revolutionists at various points. Peking is quiet.</p>
                <p>A new phase has appeared in more friendly attitude of Japan towards the Chinese
                    Government. I am informed that several shipments of arms for the revolutionists
                    have been detained by the Japanese Government. Realizing the influence of Japan
                    the high Chinese authorities appear to have made some advances. It is too early
                    to determine consequences.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Reinsch</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
